<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paulinus of York</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 644</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paulinus was a Roman missionary sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 601 to join Augustine's mission, and the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of York. He went to Northumbria with Æthelburh of Kent on her marriage to King Edwin, whom he baptised at York in 627, and preached in Deira, Bernicia and Lindsey, where he built a stone church at Lincoln. After Edwin's death in 633 he fled to Kent and ended his life as bishop of Rochester.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Paulinus was a monk from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, probably Italian by birth, sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Pope Gregory I</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mellitus">Mellitus</a> and others in the second group of the Gregorian mission, which reached Kent by 604. Little is known of his work there until he was consecrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> by Justus, archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>, and went north with Æthelburh, sister of King Eadbald of Kent. Edwin had promised that she could remain a Christian and worship as she chose. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> describes Paulinus as tall and slightly stooping, with black hair, a thin face and a hooked, thin nose.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>, Paulinus told Edwin that the birth of his daughter Eanflæd at Easter 626 was owed to his prayers, and the king promised to convert if he defeated the West Saxons, who had tried to assassinate him. He delayed until Paulinus revealed the details of a dream Edwin had had in exile at the court of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raedwald-of-east-anglia">Rædwald of East Anglia</a>. Historians think the willingness of the Northumbrian nobles and letters from Pope Boniface V also played a part. One story says Paulinus spent 36 days baptising converts at the royal palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yeavering">Yeavering</a>. Among those he baptised was Hilda, later founding abbess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/whitby-abbey">Whitby Abbey</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> was meant to be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>'s second metropolitan see, and Paulinus built a stone church there, of which no trace has been found; his church at Lincoln has been identified with the earliest phase of St Paul in the Bail. As the only Roman bishop in England, he consecrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honorius">Honorius</a> archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> after Justus died.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Edwin's death at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hatfield-chase">Battle of Hatfield Chase</a> was followed by a sharp decline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in Northumbria, and Paulinus went south with the widowed queen, leaving James the Deacon in the north. The pallium Pope Honorius I sent him arrived too late to be of use. He died at Rochester on 10 October 644 and was buried there; Ithamar, the first Englishman consecrated to a Gregorian see, succeeded him. Revered as a saint, he is venerated by the Catholic, Anglican and Eastern Orthodox churches. Historians judge his Northumbrian mission to have been largely ineffectual: the kingdom's conversion was mainly the work of Irish missionaries brought in by Oswald.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paulinus of York” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Æthelfrith's death at the River Idle in 616, the Deiran Edwin ruled both kingdoms and was baptised at York by <b>Paulinus</b> in 627, but his death at the Battle of Hatfield Chase in 633 split them again.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Edwin, son of Aelle, ruled all Northumbria from 616, accepted baptism from <b>Paulinus</b> at York in 627 and built the first church on the site of York Minster.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-deira">Kingdom of Deira</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Paulinus</b> preached at Lincoln around 628 under Northumbrian protection, building a stone church.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lindsey">Kingdom of Lindsey</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rochester-kent">Rochester</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=634" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Paulinus of York"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=634">What was here in 634 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
